From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 14:12:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: a C entry answers -1 again, because two builds measured
 it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Log 9C2045C7 (patch 0146 installed) is the first with every read and write on the stream socket
traced, and it settles the question the last build raised: after the server's whole handshake flight
(1375 bytes, four records) the title sends nothing at all — one send for the whole session, the
213-byte ClientHello, and then plain recv(512) -> -1, a RecvMsg that waits two seconds and finds
nothing, a poll every five seconds, and a Shutdown. No ClientKeyExchange, so the TLS never closes and
the game sits on its connection screen.

With 0144 the same client, at the same point, sent its 126-byte flight (ClientKeyExchange,
ChangeCipherSpec, Finished) and reached the HTTP/2 preface. The only answer that changed between the
two builds is this one: -errno (the musl convention 0145 assumed) stops it, -1 lets it continue. So
the C entries answer -1 again and keep the error for GetLastErrno.

What stays from 0145: GetLastErrno and GetLastError answer Horizon's numbering (EAGAIN 11, not this
host's 35), the poll reports a closed peer as a hang-up whatever the caller asked for, and every byte
on the wire is traced.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: Log 9C2045C7 traces every read and write on the NPLN socket: one send in the whole session (the
  213-byte ClientHello), the server's four-record flight read in one go, then a plain recv(512) -> -1, a
  RecvMsg that waits two seconds and finds nothing, a poll every five seconds and a Shutdown. The title
  never writes its ClientKeyExchange, so the TLS never closes. With 0144 (-1) the same client sent its
  126-byte flight and reached the HTTP/2 preface; the only difference is the value a failing C entry
  answers, so it answers -1 again and keeps the error for GetLastErrno. Horizon's numbering and the
  poll hang-up stay.
Issue: https://github.com/GigiFrack/NX2Apple (fork)

---
 dm/host/hle_net.c              | 13 +++++++------
 dm/host/tests/t_net_eventfd.c  |  2 +-
 dm/host/tests/t_net_loopback.c | 10 +++++-----
 dm/host/tests/t_net_tcp.c      |  4 ++--
 4 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/host/hle_net.c b/dm/host/hle_net.c
index d035e9f8..5536a229 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_net.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_net.c
@@ -181,12 +181,13 @@ static int nd_hz_errno_in(int e) {
     default: return e >= 88 && e <= 113 ? e - 50 : e;
     }
 }
-// A C entry is not the C++ one. The titles reach these through their own C library (the SDK's nnmusl),
-// whose layer takes a syscall's answer as -errno and turns it into errno = -r. Answering -1 there makes the
-// title read errno 1 (EPERM) whatever went wrong: a read that merely found nothing yet, an address family
-// this host does not serve, all of it the same "permission denied" to the caller.
-static int sk_guest(int r) { return r >= 0 ? r : -nd_hz_errno(t_skerr); }
-static int sk_guest_fail(int host_errno) { sk_error(host_errno); return -nd_hz_errno(t_skerr); }
+// A C entry answers -1 and keeps the error for GetLastErrno. The C library above it is what turns that into
+// errno, and two builds measured which answer works: sending -errno from here (the musl convention this
+// looked like) leaves the NPLN client waiting to read after the server's handshake flight, never writing its
+// own, with no error anywhere in the log; -1 has it send the ClientKeyExchange and reach the HTTP/2 preface.
+// What stays from the measurement: GetLastErrno/GetLastError answer Horizon's numbering, not this host's.
+static int sk_guest(int r) { return r >= 0 ? r : -1; }
+static int sk_guest_fail(int host_errno) { sk_error(host_errno); return -1; }
 // A stream socket's close is traced, with what went each way: a client that gives up on a connection
 // closes it, and in a log where the close is silent that reads exactly like a connection the server
 // dropped — and the two byte counts say whether the title ever spoke at all.
diff --git a/dm/host/tests/t_net_eventfd.c b/dm/host/tests/t_net_eventfd.c
index ac9f0001..008b7809 100644
--- a/dm/host/tests/t_net_eventfd.c
+++ b/dm/host/tests/t_net_eventfd.c
@@ -81,7 +81,7 @@ int main(void) {
     for (int i = 0; i < EFD_MAX; i++) assert(h_sk_Close(all[i]) == 0);
 
     // What needs the network stays offline
-    assert(h_nsk_Socket(2, 1, 0) == -nd_hz_errno(SK_ENETDOWN) && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_ENETDOWN));
+    assert(h_nsk_Socket(2, 1, 0) == -1 && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_ENETDOWN));
     sk_pollfd bad = {EFD_MAX, SK_POLLIN, 0};
     assert(h_nsk_Poll(&bad, 1, 0) == 0 && bad.revents == 0);   // one the host never made: nothing ready, as a socket with nothing to say
     sk_pollfd none = {-1, SK_POLLIN, 0x7f};
diff --git a/dm/host/tests/t_net_loopback.c b/dm/host/tests/t_net_loopback.c
index f4a27620..fb45f4aa 100644
--- a/dm/host/tests/t_net_loopback.c
+++ b/dm/host/tests/t_net_loopback.c
@@ -51,16 +51,16 @@ int main(void) {
     assert(h_nsk_Connect(self, b, 16) == 0 && h_nsk_Connect(other, a, 16) == 0);
     assert(h_nsk_Send(self, "peer", 4, 0) == 4 && h_nsk_Recv(other, data, sizeof data, 0) == 4);
     assert(h_nsk_Fcntl(other, 4, SK_NONBLOCK) == 0 && h_nsk_Fcntl(other, 3, 0) == SK_NONBLOCK);
-    assert(h_nsk_Recv(other, data, sizeof data, 0) == -nd_hz_errno(SK_EAGAIN) && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_EAGAIN));
+    assert(h_nsk_Recv(other, data, sizeof data, 0) == -1 && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_EAGAIN));
     n = 16; assert(h_nsk_GetPeerName(other, from, &n) == 0 && !memcmp(from, a, 16));
     int stranger = h_nsk_Socket(2, 2, 0); assert(stranger >= 0 && dm_sk_sendto(stranger, "drop", 4, 0, b, 16) == 4);
-    assert(h_nsk_Recv(other, data, sizeof data, 0) == -nd_hz_errno(SK_EAGAIN) && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_EAGAIN)); assert(h_sk_Close(stranger) == 0);
+    assert(h_nsk_Recv(other, data, sizeof data, 0) == -1 && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_EAGAIN)); assert(h_sk_Close(stranger) == 0);
 
     // Remote traffic and TCP never become host network activity.
     uint8_t remote[16]; address(remote, 0x08080808, 53);
     assert(dm_sk_sendto(self, "dns", 3, 0, remote, 16) == -1 && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_ENETDOWN));
-    assert(h_nsk_Connect(other, remote, 16) == -nd_hz_errno(SK_ENETDOWN) && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_ENETDOWN));
-    assert(h_nsk_Socket(2, 1, 0) == -nd_hz_errno(SK_ENETDOWN) && h_nsk_Socket(10, 2, 0) == -nd_hz_errno(SK_EAFNOSUPPORT));
+    assert(h_nsk_Connect(other, remote, 16) == -1 && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_ENETDOWN));
+    assert(h_nsk_Socket(2, 1, 0) == -1 && h_nsk_Socket(10, 2, 0) == -1);
     assert(dm_sk_sendto(self, data, SK_UDP_MAX + 1, 0, a, 16) == -1 && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_EMSGSIZE));
 
     // Poll and Select observe UDP and EventFd together, including an arrival after the wait starts.
@@ -102,7 +102,7 @@ int main(void) {
     assert(dm_sk_sendto(wildcard, "specific", 8, 0, b, 16) == 8);
     assert(h_sk_Recv(specific, data, sizeof data, SK_MSG_DONTWAIT) == 8 && !memcmp(data, "specific", 8));
     assert(h_sk_Recv(wildcard, data, sizeof data, SK_MSG_DONTWAIT) == -1 && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_EAGAIN));
-    int clash = h_nsk_Socket(2, 2, 0); assert(clash >= 0 && h_nsk_Bind(clash, b, 16) == -nd_hz_errno(SK_EADDRINUSE) && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_EADDRINUSE));
+    int clash = h_nsk_Socket(2, 2, 0); assert(clash >= 0 && h_nsk_Bind(clash, b, 16) == -1 && h_sk_GetLastError() == nd_hz_errno(SK_EADDRINUSE));
     assert(h_sk_Close(clash) == 0 && h_sk_Close(specific) == 0 && h_sk_Close(wildcard) == 0);
     puts("ok t_net_loopback"); return 0;
 }
diff --git a/dm/host/tests/t_net_tcp.c b/dm/host/tests/t_net_tcp.c
index 84ab8e65..7c303203 100644
--- a/dm/host/tests/t_net_tcp.c
+++ b/dm/host/tests/t_net_tcp.c
@@ -318,7 +318,7 @@ int main(void) {
     // The numbers the title reads: Horizon's socket errors are the Linux ones (Ryujinx Nextendo,
     // LinuxError.cs), so the host's Darwin numbering must not reach the title — a non-blocking read that
     // found nothing yet has to read as EAGAIN there, or the TLS stack calls it a hard failure. And a C entry
-    // answers -errno, which is what the title's own C library turns into errno = -r.
+    // answers -1 and keeps the error for GetLastErrno, which is what the two builds measured.
     {
         if (nd_hz_errno(SK_EAGAIN) != 11) fail("EAGAIN in the title's numbering");
         if (nd_hz_errno(ND_EINPROGRESS) != 115) fail("EINPROGRESS in the title's numbering");
@@ -329,7 +329,7 @@ int main(void) {
         if (nd_hz_errno(SK_EBADF) != 9 || nd_hz_errno(SK_EINVAL) != 22) fail("a number both ABIs share");
         if (nd_hz_errno_in(11) != SK_EAGAIN || nd_hz_errno_in(115) != ND_EINPROGRESS) fail("a title's errno read back");
         nd_msghdr empty = {0};                                  // no iovec: EINVAL, the shortest failure to reach
-        if (h_nsk_RecvMsg(ND_TCP_BASE, &empty, 0) != -22) fail("a failing C call answers -errno, not -1");
+        if (h_nsk_RecvMsg(ND_TCP_BASE, &empty, 0) != -1) fail("a failing C call answers -1");
     }
 
     // A peer that closed is polled as a hang-up, whatever the caller asked for: the NPLN client's HTTP/2
